ation um and  it will be a potentially and useful tool  not so much right now when we still  don't have a lot of  you know as testing is in access to  testing is increasing to the rapid tests  but it can be something  where we can target  people being able to download that  information in a specific community i'm  thinking in the future if we start if we  get down to a nice low level of  transmission and then we see uh  respiratory uh illness going up in a  certain community we can target that  community and say hey if you're using  the rapid test please  let us know if you're positive and so it  is an important tool that we don't yet  know all of its  how it can be used we've been talking  with colleagues in the uk in particular  who've had  rapid tests in the community available  for quite a long period of time and and  do have a very similar  voluntary reporting online and it there  are limitations to that so  we're still trying to figure out where  it's most of value  next question comes from lisa eusta city  news  hi there i'm asking a question on behalf  of a colleague and regarding long coved  i'm wondering with omicron and the  faster incubation period we're hearing  from people who are finding that at two  and a half months they're they're  feeling like they're having long coveted  and and their doctor thinks they are but  they can't get into the clinics yet  because there's a three-month  um cutoff so i'm wondering is the  criteria at this point too narrow is it  going to change  um as i guess more people have become  infected and i'm wondering you know  where you're at with that and what what  you have to say on that so there's lots  of um there's actually some really good  information that's come out of a number  of other countries particularly the uk  who started following this through their  family physician clinics primary care  clinics  so yes by definition  one doesn't meet the criteria for a long  covet until 12 weeks from your infection  so that's